<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giorgione</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1477–<a href="/years/1510">1510</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Giorgio da Castelfranco</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giorgione was a Venetian painter of the High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> from Castelfranco <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>. Very few works are securely attributed to him, among them The Tempest and the Castelfranco altarpiece, but his poetic landscapes, soft modelling and enigmatic subjects transformed Venetian painting. He worked with the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> on the frescoes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-tedeschi">Fondaco dei Tedeschi</a> and died of plague in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giorgione.jpg" alt="Giorgione" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgione · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Self_portrait,_by_Giorgio_da_Castelfranco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Giorgione's life beyond what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> records in his Lives of the Most Excellent Painters, Sculptors, and Architects. He came from Castelfranco Veneto, about 40 km inland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, and the name Giorgione may be translated as Big George. Stylistic evidence supports Carlo Ridolfi's statement that he trained in Venice under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-bellini">Giovanni Bellini</a>. In 1500 he was chosen to paint portraits of the Doge Agostino Barbarigo and the condottiere Consalvo Ferrante, and in 1504 he was commissioned to paint an altarpiece in the cathedral of Castelfranco. In 1507 and 1508 he worked on the exterior frescoes of the rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fondaco-dei-tedeschi">Fondaco dei Tedeschi</a>, having already decorated the exteriors of several Venetian palaces; very little of this work survives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Apart from the altarpiece and the frescoes, his surviving works are small paintings made for wealthy collectors to keep at home, mostly under 60 cm in either dimension. He was the first major Italian painter to concentrate on this market. In the Castelfranco Madonna the landscape background gains an importance that was new in Venetian art. He painted pictures that told no story, embodying moods of lyrical or romantic feeling, and used the soft modelling called sfumato at about the time <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> did.</p><p>The Tempest has been called the first landscape in Western painting. Its subject is unclear, and radiography shows the man at the left was once a seated female nude. The Sleeping Venus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> was left unfinished, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> is generally agreed to have painted its landscape. None of his paintings is signed and only the portrait of Laura bears a reliable date, 1 June 1506. Attributions are therefore disputed: the Pastoral Concert in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> is now widely given to Titian. Charles Hope wrote in 2012 that only three paintings have clear early evidence of his authorship, though he is generally credited with between twenty and forty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Giorgione died of the plague on 17 September 1510. He was usually thought to have died on the island of Poveglia in the Venetian lagoon, but an archival document published in 2011 places his death on Lazzareto Nuovo. Isabella d'Este asked in October 1510 for a painting by him, and the reply a month later said it was not to be had at any price. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titian">Titian</a> finished some of his paintings, though which ones remains controversial. Giorgione influenced Titian, Sebastiano del Piombo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palma</a> il Vecchio, and he was among the first Italians to paint landscapes with figures as independent pictures with no devotional, allegorical or historical purpose.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giorgione” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Venice was also one of the great artistic centres of Europe: its school of painting included Giovanni Bellini, <b>Giorgione</b>, Titian, Tintoretto and Paolo Veronese, Andrea Palladio built churches and villas that were copied for centuries, and composers from Claudio Monteverdi to Antonio Vivaldi worked in the city, which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Castelfranco Veneto, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.67,11.93&amp;year=1498" aria-label="Castelfranco Veneto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Giorgione"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 6149h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 6149h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.67,11.93&amp;year=1498">What was here in 1498? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
